{
 "suite": "features-sequences",
 "description": "SEQUENCE objects: CREATE in every accepted spelling (START WITH / START / START =, INCREMENT BY / INCREMENT / INCREMENT =, ORDER / NOORDER, COMMENT), IF NOT EXISTS and the duplicate refusal, DROP with and without IF EXISTS, NEXTVAL through the seq.NEXTVAL pseudo-column (plain, in INSERT, as a column DEFAULT applied by INSERT and by MERGE, qualified inside joined and aliased selects), SHOW / DESCRIBE SEQUENCES cells, ALTER SET INCREMENT, and the refusals Snowflake has no form for (CURRVAL in either spelling, ALTER SEQUENCE RESTART with or without a value). Converted 1:1 from features/SequencesTest (now retired). Tests that pin an EXACT allocated value skip the snowflake backend, exactly as the Java class did through its assertAllocated helper: a real account allocates per-statement ranges (START 10 INCREMENT 10 answered 10 then 1010), so only ordering, uniqueness and the within-statement step hold there - and those are pinned by allocation-properties-hold-on-both-backends, which does run live.",
 "tags": [
  "features",
  "converted"
 ],
 "tests": [
  {
   "name": "create-sequence",
   "steps": [
    {
     "sql": "CREATE SEQUENCE seq1"
    },
    {
     "sql": "SHOW SEQUENCES",
     "expect": {
      "rowCount": 1
     }
    },
    {
     "sql": "SELECT \"name\" FROM TABLE(RESULT_SCAN(LAST_QUERY_ID()))",
     "expect": {
      "value": "SEQ1"
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "name": "create-sequence-with-options",
   "steps": [
    {
     "sql": "CREATE SEQUENCE seq_custom START WITH 100 INCREMENT BY 5"
    },
    {
     "sql": "SHOW SEQUENCES",
     "expect": {
      "rowCount": 1
     }
    },
    {
     "sql": "SELECT \"name\", \"next_value\", \"interval\", \"database_name\" FROM TABLE(RESULT_SCAN(LAST_QUERY_ID()))",
     "expect": {
      "rows": [
